  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThis strategy uses a hierarchical optimization approach that optimizes the solution on multiple grids, leveraging the observation that simpler functions on coarser grids can often provide better approximations to the global optimum. The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem is non-convex, and local optimization methods can easily get stuck in poor local minima. By representing the function on different scales and transferring the best solution across scales, we can both improve the search diversity and maintain solution quality. Each grid optimization uses a combination of gradient methods and stochastic search to escape local optima. The key innovation is the three-stage multi-grid protocol: 1) Initial coarse grid random search, 2) Transition to finer grids with projection, 3) Ascending optimization chain that leverages previously found good solutions. This approach has fewer free parameters than alternatives and ensures smooth transitions between grids.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n- Representation: Piecewise constant function defined by N grid points over [0,2] with fixed intervals\n- Variables: h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for i \u2208 [0, N-1]\n- Objective: minimize C5 = max_k (dx * \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx) = max_k (dx * autocorrelation(h, 1-h)[k])\n- Constraints:\n  - \u222bh(x)dx = 1  => sum(h)*dx = 1\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe run() function orchestrates three hierarchical optimization stages:\n\nStage A: Coarse Random Initialization\n- Use 8 different random candidates on grid size 8 (sampled from uniform distribution)\n- For each candidate:\n  - Project to feasible domain (normalize sum and clip boundaries)\n  - Compute C5 score and save best candidate (lowest C5)\n- Timer checkpoint at 250 seconds\n\nStage B: Coarse Grid Expansion\n- From best candidate of Stage A, project to grid sizes [16, 32, 64, 128]\n- For each new grid size:\n  - Downscale candidate by averaging oversampling and projection\n  - Defend previous best if C5 improves\n- Use Adam-momentum stochastic search to refine each grid's best candidate\n- Timer checkpoint at 350 seconds\n\nStage C: Fine-Grid Optimization Cascade\n- Start from best candidate of Stage B\n- Optimize across 300, 400, 600 grid points using following refinements:\n  - SLSQP with aggressive alpha parameters\n  - Stochastic gradient descent with variable penalization\n  - L-BFGS-B for differentiable optimization\n- Maintain best candidate throughout chain\n- Timer checkpoint at 800 seconds\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n- Initialization: Uses 8 uniform random seeds and 2 projected initial points\n- Projection: By averaging with optimization\n- Adam momentum: mu=0.92\u00b10.03 across grids\n- Grid sizes: Starts from 8 and ascends exponentially\n- Optimization: Adam runs for 50000 steps while SLSQP uses max_iter=2500\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n- Stage C dominates timing (600-800s of 1100s budget)\n- Time checks built into optimizer warmup\n- Early exit if any grid's budget > remaining time\n- Fast projection/rejection ensures valid candidates\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n- 0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n- Sum(h)*dx \u2248 1.0 (tol=1e-6)\n- C5 computed via np.correlate with full mode and proper scaling\n- Final candidate automatically re-evaluated\n- Uses only scipy/numpy/cvxpy and provided helpers",
